\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 1\ulnone . Introduction\b0\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1 Base encoding of data is used in many situations to store or transfer data in environments that, perhaps for legacy reasons, are restricted to US-ASCII [\ul 1\ulnone ] data. Base encoding can also be used in new applications that do not have legacy restrictions, simply because it makes it possible to manipulate objects with text editors.\par
In the past, different applications have has different requirements and thus sometimes implemented base encodings is slightly different ways. Today, protocol specifications sometimes use base encodings in general, and "base64" in particular, without a precise description or reference. Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) [\ul 4\ulnone ] is often used a reference for base64 without considering the consequences for line-wrapping or non-alphaber characters. The purpose of this specification is to establish common alphabet and encoding considerations. This will hopefully reduce ambiguity in other documents, leading to better interoperability.\par
\par

\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 2\ulnone . Conventions Used in This Documents\b0\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1 The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in [\ul 2\ulnone ].\par
\par

\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 3\ulnone . Implementation Discrepancies\b0\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1 Here we discuss the discrepancies between base encoding implementations in the past and, where appropriate, madate a specific recommended behavior for the future.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\ul 3.1\ulnone . Line Feeds in Encoded Data\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 MIME [\ul 4\ulnone ] is often used as a reference for base 64 encoding. However, MIME does not define "base 64" per se, but rather a "base 64 Content-Transfer-Encoding" for use within MIME. As such, MIME enforces a limit on line length of base 64-encoded data to 76 characters. MIME inherits the encoding from Privacy Enhanced Mail (PEM) [\ul 3\ulnone ], starting that it is "virtually identical"; however, PEM uses a line length of 64 characters. The MIME and PEM limits are both due to limits within SMTP.\par
Implementations MUST NOT add line feeds to base-encoded data unless the specification referring to this document explicitly directs base encoders to add like feeds after a specific number of characters.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\ul 3.2\ulnone . Padding of Encoded Data\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 In some circumstances, the use of padding ("=") in a base-encoded data is not required or used. In the general case, when assumptions about the size of transported data cannot be made, padding is required to yield correct decoded data.\par
Implementations MUST include appropriate pad characters at the end of encoded data unless the specification referring to this document explicitly states otherwise.\par
The base64 and base32 alphabets use padding, as described below in sections \ul 4\ulnone  and \ul 6\ulnone , but the base16 alphabet does not need it; see \ul section 8\ulnone .\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\ul 3.3\ulnone . Interpretation of Non-Alphabet Characters in Encoded Data\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 Base encodings use a specific, reduces alphabet to encode binary data. Non-alphabet characters could exist within base-encoded data, caused by data corruption or by design. Non-alphabet characters may be exploited as a "covert channel", where non-protocol data can be sent for nefatious purposes. Non-alphabet characters might also be sent in order to exploint implementation errors leading to, e.g., buffer overflow attacks.\par
Implementations MUST reject the encoded data if it contains characters outside the base alphabet when interpreting base-encoded data, unless the specification referring to this document explicitly states otherwise. Such specification may instead state, as MIME does, that characters outside the base encoding alphabet should simply be ignored when interpreting data ("be liberal in what you accept"). Note that this means that any adjacent carriage return/line feed (CRLF) characters constitute "non-alphabet characters" and are ignored. Furthermore, such specifications MAY ignore the pad characters, "=", treating it as non-alphabet data, if it is present before the end of the encoded data. If more than the allowed number of pad characters is found at the end of the string (e.g., a base 64 string terminated with "==="), the excess pad characters MAY also be ignored.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\ul 3.4\ulnone . Choosing the Alphabet\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 Different applications have different requirements on the characters in the alphaber. Here are a few requirements that determine which alphabet should be used :\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-142\li994\sl240\slmult1 Handled by humans. The characters "0" and "O" are easily confused, as are "1", "l" and "I". In the base32 alphabet below, where 0 (zero) and 1 (one) are not present, a decoder may interpret 0 as O, and 1 as I or L depending on case. (However, by default it should not; see previous section.)\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Encoded into structures that madate other requirements. For base 16 and base 32, this determines the use of upper- or lowercase alphabets. For base 64, the non-alphanumeric characters (in particular, "/") may be problematic in file names and URLs.\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}Used as identifiers. Certain characters, notably "+" and "/" in the base 64 alphabet, are treated as word-breaks by legacy text search/index tools.\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 There is no universally accepted alphabet that fulfills all the requirements. For an example of a highly specialized variant, see IMAP [\ul 8\ulnone ]. In this document, we document and name some currently used alphabets.\par
\par

\pard\li284\sl360\slmult1\ul 3.5\ulnone . Canonical Encoding\par

\pard\li568\sl240\slmult1 The padding in base 64 and base 32 encoding can, if improperly implemented, lead to non-significant alterations of the encoded data. For example, if the input is only one octet for a base 64 encodings, then all six bits of the first symbol are used, but only the first two bits of the next symbol are used. These pad bits MUST be set to zero by conforming encoders, which is described in the descriptions on padding below. If this property do not hold, there is not canonical representation of base-encoded data, and multiple base-encoded string can be decoded to the same binary data. If this property (and other discussed in this document) holds, a canonical encoding is guaranteed.\par
In some environments, the alteration is critical and therefore decoders MAY chose to reject an encoding if the pad bits habe not been set to zero. The specification referring to this may mandate a specific behaviour.\par
\par

\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 4\ulnone . Base 64 Encoding\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1\b0 The following description of bae 64 is derived from [\ul 3\ulnone ], [\ul 4\ulnone ], [\ul 5\ulnone ], and [\ul 6\ulnone ]. This encoding may be referred to as "base64".\par
The Base 64 encoding is designed to represent arbitrary sequences of octers in a form that allows the use of both upper- and lowercase letters but that need not be human readalble.\par
A 65-character subset of US-ASCII is used, enabling 6 bits to be represented per printable character. (The extra 65th character, "=", is used to signify a special processing function.)\par
The encoding process represents 24-bit grou[s of input bits as output strings of 4 encoded characters. Proceeding from left to right, a 24-input group is formed by concatenating 3 8-bit input groups. These 24 bits are then treated as 4 concatenated 6-bit groups, each of which is translated into a single character in the base 64 alphabet.\par
Each 6-bit group is used as an index into an array of 64 printable characters. The character referenced by the index is placed in the output string.\par

