\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 5\ulnone . Base 64 Encoding with URL and Filename Safe Alphabet\par

\pard\li284\sl240\slmult1\b0 The Base 64 encoding with an URL and filename safe alphabet has been used in [\ul 12\ulnone ].\par
An alternative alphaben has been suggested that would use "~" as the 63rd character. Since the "~" character has special meaning in some file system environments, the encoding described in this section is recommended instead. The remaining unreserved URI character is ".", but some fule system environments fo not permit multiple "." in a filename, thus making the "." character unattractive as well.\par
The pad character "=" is typically percent-encoded when used in an URI [\ul 9\ulnone ], but if the data length is known implicitly, this can be avoided by skipping the padding; see \ul section 3.2\ulnone .\par
This encoding may be referred to as "base64url". This encoding should not be regarded as the same as the "base64" encoding and should not be referred to as only "base64". Unless clarified otherwise, "base64" refers to the base 64 in the previous section.\par

