\pard\sl360\slmult1\ul\b 8\ulnone . Base 16 Encoding\par

\pard\li142\sl240\slmult1\b0 The following description is original but analogous to previous descriptions. Essentially, Base 16 encoding is the standard case-insensitive hex encoding and may be referred to as "base16" or "hex".\par
A 16-character subset of US-ASCII is used, enabling 4 bits to be represented per printable character.\par
The encoding process represents 8-bit groups (octets) of input bits as output strings of 2 encoded characters. Proceeding from left to right, an 8-bit input is taken from the input data. These 8 bits are then treated as 2 concatenated 4-bit groups, each of which is translated into a single character in the base 16 alphabet.\par
Each 4-bit group is used as an index into an array of 16 printable characters. The character referenced by the index is placed in the output string.\par

